    <header>
      <h1>Build a case narrative where every sentence traces back to the record</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        PAi Legal assembles facts, statements and allegations into a structured narrative, each unit tied to the document that supports it. Every matter stays in its own storage location on hardware you control. No account, no upload, no server holding the file.
      </p>
      <a class="btn" href="https://apps.microsoft.com/detail/9NP4XL1MRXP8">View in Microsoft Store</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="pai-claims.html">See PAi Claims</a>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The narrative is built from the record, one unit at a time</h2>
      <p>
        Most case software stores documents. The hard part of a case is the narrative — the ordered account of what happened and why that entitles you to something — and the fact that every sentence of it has to survive being challenged.
      </p>
      <p>
        PAi Legal builds that account as a structured object. Each fact, statement and allegation is entered as a discrete unit and tied to what supports it: a document, a page, a photograph, a dated communication. Sentences assemble into paragraphs, paragraphs into the narrative, and every level retains the link back to its source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Unsupported assertions become visible. A claim with nothing behind it stands out as a unit with no source — precisely the sentence opposing counsel will find first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The case map: where the record concentrates, and where it doesn’t</h2>
      <p>
        Once facts, documents and allegations are linked, the connections form a map. Every element carries a weight derived from its relationships: how much of the record connects to it, how many paths run through it. The values are computed deterministically from the evidence you entered, not learned from training data.
      </p>
      <p>
        The useful half of that map is the low-pressure end. A part of the case with little connected support is a structural gap. PAi Legal names them. The least-represented part of the record is surfaced explicitly as the first place to inspect while there is still time to shore it up or drop the argument.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why local matters more in legal work</h2>
      <p>
        Client files sitting on a third-party service are a copy you do not control, held under terms you did not negotiate, potentially reachable by process directed at the vendor. Confidentiality obligations do not get easier to satisfy by adding a custodian.
      </p>
      <p>
        PAi Legal loads its AI model onto your machine during setup and runs it there. After installation it works with no network connection, no account, and no data leaving the computer. The AI is optional: import, OCR, organization and search all work without a model loaded.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>One matter, one location — chosen by you</h2>
      <p>
        Each case is stored where you put it: a removable drive, a specific volume, a designated path. Not a row in a shared database with a permission flag. For a firm, ethical walls can be implemented as physical separation rather than software permissions. A matter under litigation hold can be preserved by preserving the volume it lives on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the software does</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Traceable narrative</h3>
          <p>Facts, statements and allegations are entered as discrete units tied to their sources, then assembled into the account.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Case map</h3>
          <p>The links between facts and documents are drawn as a map, weighted by how much of the record connects to each point.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Gap detection</h3>
          <p>The least-supported part of the record is surfaced explicitly so thin arguments are visible before an opponent finds them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Document import with OCR</h3>
          <p>PDFs, Word files, spreadsheets, Outlook mail and scanned or photographed paper become searchable text. OCR is built in.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Separate matters</h3>
          <p>Distinct legal matters stay apart inside one case folder instead of blurring into a single undifferentiated dispute.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Optional private AI</h3>
          <p>A language model that runs on your own hardware. Nothing is sent anywhere.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>For firms, solos and self-represented litigants</h2>
      <p>
        At first run you choose whether you are practicing or handling your own matter. The self-represented mode uses plain language and explains every term as it appears. Attorney mode uses standard terminology without the explanations. The document handling, matter separation and local-only guarantee are identical either way.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Working with PAi Claims</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="pai-claims.html" style="color:var(--accent);">PAi Claims and PAi Legal share a case</a>, so claim evidence carries into the legal workspace when a denial turns into a dispute rather than being rebuilt from scratch. Nothing is re-entered, re-scanned or rebuilt.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Is PAi Legal for lawyers or for self-represented litigants?</h3>
        <p>Both. At first run you choose which you are. The underlying features are the same; only the language changes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What does it cost?</h3>
        <p>The download is free and so is the first case, which runs on the full product.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How does it find weak points?</h3>
        <p>The links between facts and documents form a weighted map. The least-supported part is surfaced as the first structural gap to inspect.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Does any data leave my computer?</h3>
        <p>No. After the optional model download, everything runs locally. There is no account and no upload.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="notice">
      PAi Legal is an organizational and analytical aid. It is not a law firm or a substitute for licensed professional advice. Verify deadlines, legal interpretations and any generated document before relying on it.
    </div>

